/** \page concept The concept of yaxt

  \tableofcontents

  \section intro Introduction
  \section concept Concept

  The usage of yaxt can be divided into five steps:
    - defining the source and target decomposition
    - generating a data type independent mapping between the source and target decompositions
    - generating a data type dependent redistribution object
    - doing the data exchange(s)
    - cleaning up the generated yaxt objects

  \subsection decomp Definie decomposition

  At first one has to assign a unique index value to each unique data value of a
  decomposition. For example in a 1D array one could assign the index values 0 to n-1 to the
  elements of the array. Once this is done, the user has to generate one each process an
  index list (\ref Xt_idxlist) containing all indices of the data values that are available on
  the process. The order of the indices has to match the order of the respective values in the
  actual data array.\n
  The yaxt library has a varity of methods to generate index list (see \ref idxlists).

  \subsection xmap Generate mapping

  With the definition of the decomposition of a source and target domain you can generate an
  exchange map (\ref Xt_xmap). The exchange map matches the source and target decompositions
  of all processes and generates a mapping between the decomposition and respective processes.\n
  There are multiple algorithms available to generate an exchange map (see \ref xmaps).

  \subsection redist Generate redistribution object

  An exchange map can be used to generate a redistribution object (\ref Xt_redist). Based on the
  MPI data type passed to the constructure of the redistribution object, an object will be created
  that is able to redistribute data of the given type, which is distributed according to the
  source decomposition into the target decomposition. The provided MPI data type allows the
  redistribution of data with an arbitrary data layout.\n
  It is possible to merge multiple redistribution object into a single one (see \ref redist_colls).

  \subsection exchange Do exchange

  The exchange can be executed by a blocking call to \ref xt_redist_s_exchange or
  \ref xt_redist_s_exchange1 (only possible for non-merged redistributions). It can be called
  repeatedly for as many times as necessary.

  \subsection cleanup Clean up

  Once any yaxt object is not directly needed anymore it can be deleted by the respective delete
  calls (e.g. \ref xt_idxlist_delete).
 */
